@@ -45,8 +47,10 @@ $\sum_{k \ge 1} 1/(k H_k^{2})$, with $H_k = 1 + 1/2 + \dots + 1/k$.
 **What the pair shows.** The harmonic series already diverges as slowly as any
 explicit series on this page, and dividing its terms by the running total
 produces something that diverges more slowly still. Dividing by the square of the
-running total overshoots into convergence. So the two exponents $1$ and $2$
-straddle the boundary, and there is no last divergent series in the family.
+running total overshoots into convergence. So exponent $1$ gives a divergent
+member and exponent $2$ a convergent one. The absence of a slowest divergent
+positive series comes from applying Abel-Dini again to the newly produced
+divergent series, not from a last-exponent claim about this fixed pair.
 
 ## Facts & Assumptions
 
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-17-{"id":"thm-kummer-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Thomson–Bruckner–Bruckner Theorem 3.37 gives exactly the positive-weight Kummer expression, the positive-liminf convergence criterion, and the eventual-nonpositive plus divergent reciprocal-weight criterion. The proof follows the sourced telescoping comparison, expanded with extended-real tail-infimum details.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-18-{"id":"cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Both textbooks explicitly identify d'Alembert's ratio test as Kummer's test with constant weights. The item states only the two limsup/liminf ratio conclusions recovered in the positive-term setting and proves them through reciprocal bounds, so it is an expanded semantic specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-19-{"id":"cor-raabe-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state Raabe's positive-term test and identify it as Kummer with harmonic weights. The item uses k+1 rather than k because its naturals start at zero, and uses liminf/limsup rather than assuming a single limit. The proof is the sourced Kummer specialization with that convention delta.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-20-{"id":"thm-gauss-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests"],"rationale":"The sources state Gauss's test for a_n/a_{n+1}=1+alpha/n+O(n^{-beta}) with beta>1, convergent exactly for alpha>1. The item's bound |r_k|<=C k^{-1-epsilon}, epsilon positive rational, is the same big-O regime after choosing beta=1+epsilon, with local rational-power conventions. Its borderline proof is a locally constructed summable-error product argument rather than the sourced logarithmic/Kummer proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"thm-abel-dini","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The source states the divergent-series Abel–Dini–Pringsheim theorem for positive terms with inclusive partial sums S_n: sum a_n/S_n diverges and sum a_n/S_n^t converges for t>1, hence in particular at t=2. These are exactly the item's two claims and convention. The local proof derives the two estimates directly from Cauchy and telescoping facts.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"rem-strength-order-of-the-nonnegative-tests","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html","https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The source set establishes the ratio-root strength order, Kummer specializations, Raabe and Gauss criteria, and the Abel–Dini no-final-comparison obstruction. The item is a repository-specific synthesis of those results and local availability constraints. It also contains one inaccurate alteration about a 1/(k log k) Gauss remainder, recorded as an A2 falsehood; as a Remark it has no separate proof component.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"fs-nth-term-test-converse","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter places the divergent harmonic series immediately after the nth-term condition precisely to show that terms tending to zero are not sufficient. The item turns that standard failed converse into an explicit false statement and refutes it with the same family under the local index convention. The numbered refutation is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests","https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html"],"rationale":"The sourced ratio test requires liminf>1 for divergence, while the CSUDH notes give oscillating-ratio geometric witnesses showing why limsup cannot replace it. The item formulates the pedagogical false alteration and uses a closely related bounded oscillating multiplier. The detailed computation is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.provenance.jsonl:25:{"id":"fs-universal-comparison-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The Abel–Dini–Pringsheim theorem explicitly constructs, from any divergent positive series, another divergent positive series with smaller terms; this rules out a slowest divergent series and any single universal comparison series. The item presents the negation as a false claim and follows the sourced construction b_n/B_n with local quantifiers made explicit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
